variants.reconstruction:
%n mimma | šēru | ina nam[āri]
#note: See @bib{RN117@300}: “In the SB text this line becomes @i{mimmû šēri ina namāri}, and is standard repertoire in the second half of the epic (see Chapter 13, the commentary on SB VII 90).”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 90

%n ana pān (|) šarūrī | ša šamaš || illak[ā | dim]ā[šu]
// L I.4 SB "VII" 92
// cf. L I.4 SB "VI" 83

%n amhurka | šamaš || aššum ṣayyādi | hābi[li (|) am]ēli
#note: See @bib{RN117@300}: “The word @i{amhurka} utilizes the ‘performative’ preterite (for this in Gilgameš see SB III 11 and commentary).”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 93

%n ana lāʾiṭi | ša lā ušamṣan[ni] || mal[a | ibrīya]
#note: See @bib{RN117@300}: “The verb @i{lâṭu} is commonly used to describe the function of a @i{rappu}, a kind of wooden shackle, perhaps a neck-stock, with which prisoners-of-war could be restrained (on @sux{rab ri.ri} = @i{rappu lāʾiṭu} see @bib{RN124@304}). The use here of the participle, apparently to describe the hunter, suggests that the @i{rappu} was also an instrument used in hunting.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VII" 94-95

%n lū harṣ[a]t | zittašu | ina mahr[ī]ka
#note: See @bib{RN117@300–301}: “Gadd and Lambert both copied the first two signs of the line as @akk{GIŠ.HUR}. Landsberger interpreted these as @akk{<li>-iṣ-hir} (properly @i{ḫír}), and thus sought a line different from that preserved in the SB text. However, there seems to me (and to Westenholz) to be more to the first sign than three wedges. Accordingly I am encouraged to read the text in the same way as the SB, though in doing so one is forced to accept the spelling @i{ar-sa-at} for @i{harṣat} (cf. a comparable spelling in l. 16). To my eyes the sign before @i{ah}, later in the line, is unrecognizable as it stands; I assume that it is @i{ma} written over a partly erased @i{ah}, and again seek to reconcile the MB text with the SB version.”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 98

%n alkī | šamhat || šīmata | lušī[mk]i
#note: See @bib{RN117@300–301 ad 11–13}: “For a parallel to the formula used to introduce the curse see the passage of Ištar’s Descent quoted in Chapter 10, the introduction to SB Tablet VII (on 100–5). The form @i{šīmata}, with epenthetic vowel, is literary for @i{šīmta} (see Chapter 9, the section on Some features of language and style). The rare word @i{izziru}, the counterpart of the SB version’s @i{izru}, appears to be an *@i{ipris} formation from √@i{nzr} (see @i{AHw}, p. 1564).”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 102
// cf. L I.8 SB "-" 103

%n luzzurki | izzira | rabâ
// L I.4 SB "VII" 104
// cf. L I.8 SB "-" 103

%n ē tušbī | ina kussî | ša ardāti
#note: See @bib{fromebllab0039@106-107}: ““May you not sit on the young women’s chair!” Previous attempts at restoring the parallel line in the MB Ur MS (UET 6/2 394) include @akk{E₂.EŠ₃.DAM]} (@bib{RN1419Gadd@109}) @i{bīt} @akk{qe₂#-re#-et#} @i{ardāti} (@bib{lambert1992prostitution@129}), and @akk{ma#-aš#-<ta>-ki# ša#} @i{ardāti} (?) (@bib{RN117@302}). The SB parallel suggests reading the Ur tablet as @akk{{giš#}GU#.[Z]A ša#}, which seems compatible with George’s copy (@bib{RN117@pl. 22}), as well as with the digital photograph (@url{http://www.ur-online.org/subject/55645/}).” See also @bib{RN117@301–302}: “This curse ensures the ostracism of the prostitute from the company of her peers. In the context of the preceding line, it excludes her from the normal family life which provided Babylonian women with social position and protection. There are four badly damaged signs between @akk{i+na} and @akk{KI.SIKIL{meš}}, only the last of which is readily identifiable: though it has sustained some interior damage, the outline shape of it fits either @i{it} or @i{ša} (note that SB has @akk{in[a x] ša₂}). From the point of view of sense, Gadd’s é.[éš(for èš).dam] is not likely, since the @i{aštammu} is the typical haunt of the prostitute, not of respectable family women. Landsberger read é-x-x. Lambert’s reading, @i{bīt} ⸢@i{qí-re-et}⸣, is not wholly borne out by the traces: the second and third signs are not compatible in my view. In Babylonian literature the archetypal place of abode of young women is the @i{maštaku} (MB @i{maltaku}), the private part of the house to which the only man granted automatic access was the husband. No other building or chamber is so repeatedly associated with @i{ardatu} (@bib{RN2759@15, ll. 36, 38, 40}: @i{Ardat lilî} incantation; @i{LKU} 43 obv. 6; @bib{RN752@224, 8}: syncretistic hymn to Nanāy; @i{CT} 16 9 i 26–7: @i{Udugḫul} IV; MacMillan, @i{BA} V, p. 620, rev. 20–1 // @i{SBH} 58, 14–15; cf. @bib{RN230@618}). However, this word is only possible if one emends to @akk{ma#-aš#-<ta>-ki# ša#} @i{ardāti}. It may be that what the scribe actually wrote was ⸢@i{ma-aš-qí-it}⸣ @i{ardāti}. Though young women no doubt frequented watering places daily, to draw water and socialize, nevertheless @i{mašqīt} does not sit happily with the qualification @i{ardāti}, and it seems to me that banning the prostitute from the local well, as it were, would not be the most direct way of describing her exclusion from the company of respectable women. For the moment it is safest to conclude that the traces remain without a secure decipherment.”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 108

%n ṣubātki! | damqa || qaqqaru | lišehhi
#note: See @bib{RN117@302}: “It has been customary to read the first word @i{su-nu-ki}, following Gadd’s original decipherment. In a MB text which observes regular inflections, I am not convinced that @i{sūnuki}, ‘your lap’, can be accusative, unless one takes it as a deviant form of hymno-epic @i{sūnuk}. The second line of the couplet is concerned with clothing, and so to my mind a garment of some kind is best expected in the first. The adjective @i{damqu} is in any case not common for a part of the body, let alone the prostitute’s lap, but it is the conventional qualification of good-quality fabric (note especially @i{ṣu-ba-tu damqu}(sig₅) in the synonym list @i{CT} 18 11 ii 16–17). Although @i{sūnu} can designate a garment in administrative texts, in literature it is very rare in such usage. Accordingly I read @i{su-bat-ki} as erroneous for @i{ṣubātki}, and see in the spelling the same phonetic phenomenon that was observed in l. 8, @i{har-sa-at} for @i{harṣat}. The third word in the line was read @akk{qa-du#-tu[m]}, ‘mud; sediment, (beer) dregs’, by Landsberger, Foster and Lambert. To my eyes the second sign cannot be @i{du} but is very possibly another @i{qa} (Westenholz’s copy agrees), which makes the word @i{qaqqaru} the most likely candidate for restoration. The ground soils the prostitute’s finery because, on occasions when more comfortable accommodation is lacking, it is there that she must ply her trade (as also implied in l. 24). In both lines of this couplet the poet draws attention to the irony of the prostitute’s situation: though her job requires her to look attractive, it can also require her to get her finery dirty.”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 109

%n lubār (|) isinnāti | šakrum || ina tu[r]buʾi | liballil
#note: See @bib{RN117@302}: “Foster reads the first word @akk{lub?-ši}, but what remains of the first sign rules this out; @i{lubār isinnāti} is also found in SB VIII 48. The penultimate word was tentatively read @akk{tu-[r]e!?-e!;-[r]u?-u₂} by von Soden, @i{AHw}, p. 1373, s.v. @i{tūrû}, a hapax legomenon which he derived from @i{arû}, ‘to vomit’. However, the traces seem to rule this out: there is space for four signs, not three, and the third of these seems to have the right shape for BU (cf. Lambert’s copy). Thus the sordid picture of the drunkard puking over the hapless prostitute must be replaced by the graphic image of her rolling around with her drunken customer in a cloud of @i{turbuʾu}, the dust stirred up in the course of conducting her business in the open.”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 110
// cf. L I.8 SB "-" 108

%n ē taršî | bīt (|) unâti
#note: See @bib{RN117@302 ad 18–19}: “Though @i{bīt unâti} can be a storeroom for household equipment and furniture (as in the title of the functionary @i{šatam bīt unâti}), the point of this couplet is surely that the prostitute will not have a house full of dishes, pots and pans, and the nice things a housewife traditionally likes to acquire (cf. l. 14). In this regard it is interesting that counterpart of the simple @i{bīt unâti} in the SB text is [@i{bīt}? ...] @i{u banâti}, ‘[@i{a house full of} ...] and lovely things’ (l. 111). The first word of the second line seems to be in apposition to @i{bīt unâti}, despite being nominative, but I have not found a suitable decipherment. The SB version is again preserved only in the latter half of the line but seems to be different here too.”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 111

%n ... | [...] lū ... ella | ay irši
#note: See @bib{RN117@302}: “What precedes @i{ella ay irši} is all very doubtful; the line does not tally well with the late version, which is, in any case, also only partly preserved.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VII" 113

%n ... [... p]aššūru | šumuh (|) nišī | bāltum | ay innadi | ina bītīki?
#note: See @bib{RN117@303}: “The phrase @akk{šu-muh UN{meš}} elsewhere occurs as an epithet of cereal crops, @akk{{d}nisaba} (@bib{RN432@158, 16}: Tamarisk and Date-Palm); here, together with @i{baltu}, it apparently refers to food set out on the tray or table (cf., in the late version, @i{tar}]@i{āṣ paššūri}: SB VII 114). The traces at the end of the line favour ⸢@i{ina bīti-ki}⸣ as much as ⸢@i{i}+@i{na bīti-ki}⸣ (see below on l. 28). As a whole this line looks much too long, and one might have expected a division after @i{šumuh nišī}. However, the line is retained as a single one (with omission of @i{bāltu}) in the SB version (l. 114). The significance of the line appears to be that the prostitute, shunned by society, will not find guests prepared to eat in her company (cf. l. 33).
// L I.4 SB "VII" 114

%n d[i]nnût (|) lalêki | lū dakkan (|) ... [...]
#note: See @bib{RN117@303}: “The word @i{dinnūtu} is treated in @i{Hh} IV 147 and @i{Nabnītu} IV 209: @sux{{giš}na₂.aš.na₂} = @akk{din-nu-tum} (@i{MSL} V, p. 163; cf. XVI, p. 84). The significance of the Sumerian term appears to be that this is a bed for single occupancy, and its use in the present context thus implies that the @i{dakkannu} is the place where the prostitute is to sleep, rather than where she entertains clients. The term @i{dakkannu}, in Sumerian @sux{da.ga.na} and @sux{daggan}, is most recently discussed by P.-A. Beaulieu, who translates ‘room, bedroom, private quarters’ (@bib{beaulieu1992new@101 ff.}). It is something typically occupied by women and old men. Its association in @i{Šurpu} with parts of the door has encouraged the translations ‘Türöffnung’ (@i{AHw}) and ‘porch’, but, like its Sumerian counterpart, it can be a place where one sleeps. In the NB document from Uruk which prompted Beaulieu’s discussion, a @i{dakkannu} is occupied by a temple slave (@bib{beaulieu1992new@99} = @i{YOS} XIX 110, 7). That is the point here: the prostitute must make do with very inferior sleeping accommodation. In the present context the @i{dakkannu} appears to be what she sleeps on, rather than in, and a translation ‘bedroom’ would be too broad.”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 115

%n išpallurtī | pahārim | lū mūšabūk[i]
#note: See @bib{RN117@303}: “The ‘crossroads of the potter’ refers to a junction of roads in the potter’s quarter (so Foster), probably traditionally a poor neighbourhood, and maybe outside the urban area proper, near the clay-pits (cf. Oppenheim’s remarks in @bib{RN729@41, fn. 4}).”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 116
// cf. L I.8 SB "-" 107

%n hurbā[t]um | lū mayyālak[i]
#note: See @bib{RN117@303}: “Gadd and Landsberger read the first word @akk{har-ba-tu₄}. With Foster and Lambert I follow @i{CAD} in reading @akk{hur-ba-tum}, the plural of @i{huribtu}. Von Soden now analyses it as the plural of the adjective @i{harbu} I (@i{AHw}, p. 1559); on @i{harbātu}, ‘uncultivated land’, see recently @bib{stol1988old@173} (sc. @i{eqlētu}?). The form @i{mayyālaki}, with epenthetic vowel, is literary for @i{mayyālki} but results in an unsatisfactory dactyl at the end to the line; the SB text offers a trochee, the plural @i{maṣallūka}.”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 117a

%n ṣilli (|) dūri | lū manzāzūk[i]
#note: See @bib{RN117@303}: “The shaded ground below the city wall was a traditional place for the prostitute to attract customers. See the orgiastic OB cult-song of Ištar: @akk{eṭ-lu-ut a-li-ku-nu pu-uh-hi-ra-nim-ma a-na ṣi-il-li du-ri-im i ni-lik}, ‘gather together the young men of your city and let us go to the shade of the wall’ (@bib{soden1991spaet@340, 13–14}); for a parallel passage in Ištar’s Descent see Chapter 10, the introduction to SB VII (on 102–4).”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 117b
// L I.8 SB "-" 106

%n [šakr]u | u ṣamû || limha<ṣū> | lē[tki]
// L I.4 SB "VII" 119
// L I.8 SB "-" 108

%n [... | bē]lti (|) dīni! || el[īki | lil]si
#note: See @bib{RN117@303}: “The emendation relies on SB VII 120. For @i{šasû} with the nuance of ‘to make a claim in law, sue’, see @i{AHw}, p. 1196, G 13 (cf. @i{CAD} Š/2, p. 155, ‘to summon’ in legal contexts). The person who sues the prostitute is female (@i{bēlti dīni}).”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 120

%n [ūr (|) bītīki || ay isē]r | i[tinnu]
#note: See @bib{RN117@303–304}: “The restoration at the very beginning of the line is traditional, going back to Landsberger. The evidence for the roof as the object of the verb @i{sêru} is now collected in @i{CAD} S, p. 228 (see also @bib{AbB_12@17}); @i{igar bīti-ki} is also possible, of course (cf. @bib{RN216@130, 77}). A dissenting voice is Foster, who reads the verb as @i{i-ṣì-ir} < @i{eṣēru}, ‘to design’), employing a most unusual value (@bib{RN163@38}).”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 121

%n [ina ... | l]irbiṣū | ka[lbū]
#note: See @bib{RN117@304}: “Though the SB text has @akk{l]i-ir-bi-ṣi qa-du-u₂}, it is noteworthy that @i{rabāṣu} is not a verb associated with birds, and I am not convinced that we are constrained to force the later text on to the broken word preserved here. To me the sign UR suggests not so much a variation of @sux{uru.hul.a{mušen}} = @i{qadû} (cf. Lambert) as dogs (i.e. @akk{UR.[GI₇{meš}]} = @i{kalbū}; so also Foster). Dogs are commonly the subject of @i{rabāṣu}.”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 122

%n [ša lubār (|) taki]lti | lū ... [...]
#note: See @bib{RN117@304}: “The first sign of the last word was read @i{taš} by Gadd, Landsberger, von Soden (@i{AHw}, p. 1338, s.v. @i{tašḫītu}, ‘Schwängerung(?)’), and Foster, but @i{mal} by Lambert (@i{mal-ṭí-tum}, ‘dedicated thing’). I could only see four wedges, three upright and one horizontal, and am not persuaded that either @i{taš} or @i{mal} is right. Instead one might take it as an incomplete @i{ri}, which produces two alternative decipherments, @i{reḫētu}, ‘remnants’, and @i{riḫītu}, ‘something engendered or produced, creation’. But while the text remains in such poor condition and the SB version is also in a fragmentary state, it cannot be certain which decipherment is correct, if any.”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 127

%n [... | sūn]i | šuhhû | lū nidin (|) ...
#note: See @bib{RN117@304}: “Lambert restored [@i{ú-lap su-n}]@i{i} here, which makes good sense, though of course not just prostitutes, but all women, have to endure @i{ulāp sūni šuḫḫû}, ‘a soiled sanitary towel’. This restoration seems to be based on the late text, which at this point reads @akk{x-lap su-ni šah-[hu-u₂ ...]} (SB VII 128). However, the difficulty is that x ≠ @i{ú}: see further Chapter 13, the commentary ad loc.” Y. Cohen (private communication) suggests that @i{nidin} in this context may denote the wages of a prostitute. For further discussion on the use of @i{nidnu} specifically in the sense of “prostitute’s wages,” see @bib{cohen2015wages}. 
// cf. L I.4 SB "VII" 128-129

%n [ašsum | yâ]ši || ella | tušemṭinni
#note: See @bib{RN117@304 ad 39–40}: “Enkidu’s purity in the wild was sexual (for @i{ellu} in this meaning see the references collected by @bib{RN609@65, fn. 160}). Note in this regard that in the late epic the verb used to record the loss of Enkidu’s virginity, for which he here so savagely upbrades the prostitute, is @i{šuḫḫû}, ‘to defile’; see further Chapter 13, the commentary on SB I 199; the verb used here, @i{šumṭû}, is also found in that episode, though in the II/1 stem (SB I 201; on the parallelism in vocabulary, which is also yields a correspondance MB @i{ella} : SB @i{ullula}, see further the commentary on SB I 199). The implicit purity of the virgin male is well known from exorcistic rituals that require the participation of ‘a youth who has never known a woman’ (@akk{{lu₂}}@i{ṣuḫāru}(tur) @i{šá sinništa}(munus) @i{là īdû}(zu): @url{https://www.ebl.lmu.de/fragmentarium/SU-1951.1}{STT 73}, passim; @url{https://www.ebl.lmu.de/fragmentarium/Sm.761}{AMT 61 no. 5}, 12).”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 130

%n [ultu ullânum-ma | tuk]ku || ultu šamê | išassašum-ma
#note: See @bib{RN117@304}: “The restoration comes from the SB version, IV 195 [= V 96] and VII 133. It is possible that @i{tukku} represents a late expansion not present in MB Ur, in which case the trace before @i{ul-tu} is of @i{ullânum-m}]@i{a}.”
// L I.4 SB "V" 96
// L I.4 SB "VII" 133

%n ša ištēn | bēr || limhaṣ | šaparšu
#note: See @bib{RN117@304 ad 51–52}: “As noted by Foster, these lines describe on the one hand the frustration of the prostitute’s potential client, while yet a league off, and on the other the eager preparations that mark, while even further away, his excited anticipation of an encounter with her. For the phrase @i{šapra maḫāṣu}, see also @bib{gruber1980aspects@380 ff.}, who views it as expressing acute disappointment or chagrin. Here it is more a gesture of angry frustration, as also in Ištar’s Descent, where it is the reaction of Ereškigal on discovering that she has been tricked by the cunning Aṣûšu-namer. For @i{qimmata nussusu} see SB VI 2, where it is what Gilgameš does on returning to Uruk from the Cedar Forest, and one of the toiletry preparations that transform him from a dirty traveller into the object of Ištar’s desire. The implication of the distances mentioned in these lines is that the client is a traveller on the road, and the poet thus acknowledges that men away from home, whether commercial agents or military men, can be enthusiastic patrons of prostitutes.”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 154

%n liddinki | ṣupra | u kišāda
#note: See @bib{RN117@304–305 ad 54–55}: “This couplet describes the jewellery and trinkets with which the appreciative customer showers a good prostitute. According to Lambert, ‘the hoof, or claw, is a manufactured item of jewellery’, though the dictionaries indicate that @i{ṣupru} is usually a decorative ornamentation at the foot of legs of furniture or at the end of a handle. However, the horde of jewellery from the harem tombs recently found under Aššurnaṣirpal II’s NW palace at Nimrud includes sets of jewellery for the hand, comprising four finger rings each joined by a chain of worked gold to a wrist bracelet (two exemplars can be seen in @bib{damerji1998graeber@32, fig. 40, middle right}). The effect is of gold fingers or talons stretching like a hen’s foot over the back of the hand, and this might conceivably be what is meant by @i{ṣupru}. An alternative solution might be that the well-off prostitute, and maybe other women, adorned their fingernails (@i{ṣupru}) with gold-leaf or some other expensive material. It may be significant, though, that the SB version has a different text, reading instead @i{ṣurra uqnâ u ḫurāṣa} (l. 157). One motive for altering a received text is a failure to understand what it means. The word @i{tur-tu-ri} appears in the SB version and elsewhere as @i{tu}(@i{t})@i{turrū}, a term most recently discussed by @bib{NABU1992-34@34}, who presents evidence from Mari that it means ‘ornements annexes’, revising his earlier view that it signified granulated decoration (@bib{RN2625@231–2}). As a qualification of ear-rings this word probably signifies an elaborate piece of jewellery with several pendants or other component parts. Either the scribe of MB Ur has inadvertently reversed the first two signs of what may have been an unfamiliar word or the word is a reduplication, < *@i{turtur}. The word is either a second object of @i{mullû} or it qualifies @i{anṣabtu} as a genitive. Either way @i{anṣabtu} is a false form; with @i{limellâ} we expect the dual in status rectus or constructus (both @i{anṣabtā}). It is not the only grammatical mistake in this line. Von Soden already anticipated the new, but admittedly obvious, decipherment @i{uznīka} (‘lege er an deine Ohren’). The masculine possessive suffix is a bad howler, but one that is repeated with @i{áš-šu-mi-ka} in l. 58.”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 157

%n ana eṭli | ša kunnu | kunūnūšu || išpi[kkūšu | ša]pkū
#note: See @bib{RN117@305}: “The reading @i{ku-nu-nu-šu} seems unavoidable: from the traces of the first sign [@i{k}]@i{i-nu-nu-šu} does not look possible. I agree with Lambert in viewing this, a hapax legomenon, as a variant of @i{ganūnu}, which, as well as storehouse, also denotes the home where one’s family lives (as in Erra IV 84, 99, 102) and even the household or family itself (@i{LKA} 52, 19). The word before it was read @i{ku-un-n}[@i{u-ku}] by Lambert, but to my eyes the damaged traces of the end of this word are not compatible with such a reading: though not absolutely clear, the third and fourth signs seem to be the same shape, i.e. @i{ku-un-nu-nu}. If this decipherment is correct the scribe is guilty of dittography. The @i{ganūnu kunnu} is a secure, permanent family home, exactly what the prostitute lacks when she has no wealthy patron, as the curses have earlier repeatedly stated. Here, if we take ll. 56–8 together, a career is envisaged for her in which, through the intervention of Ištar, the patron of prostitutes, she attracts the devotion of a rich man who installs her in his house and divorces his first wife, the mother of his sons. For the poet this entry into family life in a secure domestic environment marks the most fortunate potential achievement of the common prostitute.”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 159

%n ištar | lēʾât (|) ilī | lišēribki
#note: See @bib{RN117@305}: “For Ištar’s epithet ‘most able of the gods’ see, for example, @bib{scheil1918poeme@176, 12}: @i{le-i-it i-li} (Agušaya); @i{KAR} 144, 13: @i{le-e-it ilī}@akk{{meš}} @i{rabûti}@akk{{meš}} (incantation); cf. @i{BMS} 32 = @bib{RN1289@122, 14}: @i{le-ʾ-at} [...] (incantation); @i{KAR} 158 i 15: @i{le-a-at a-bi-ša} (song incipit).”
// L I.4 SB "VII" 160

%n uš[tabba]l | mimmu (|) kabtatīšu
#note: See @bib{RN117@305}: “@i{mimmu}, a rare form of the construct state of @i{mimmû}, already occurs in OB letters (@i{CT} 6 7a, 7: @i{a-na mi-mu bīt}(é) @i{a-bi-šu-nu}; @i{YOS} II 111, 16: @i{mi-im-mu tu-ša-ad-di-nu}) and in MB (boundary stone of Melišiḫu: @i{MDP} II 104 iii 45: @i{mim-mu id-di-nu-šu}). In SB it is commoner (@bib{RN432@265, rev. 8}: níg.nam dingir.ra // @i{mim-mu ili}, ‘the property of a god’; in omen apodoses: @bib{RN61@83, 116–17}: @i{mim-mu ili}(dingir)/@i{šarri}(lugal); @i{KAR} 376 rev. 11: @i{mim-mu bīti}(é) @i{šâti}(bi)), though some references may be the NB indefinite @i{mimmu} < @i{mimma}.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "VII" 163
// cf. L I.4 SB "VII" 164

%n m[i]mmu | ibrī || ina šunat (|) mušītīya
// cf. L I.4 SB "I" 246
// L I.4 SB "VII" 165

%n i[lsû]-ma | šamû || qaqqaru | īpul
#note: See @bib{RN117@305}: “This line recalls the very similar one that introduces the description of Gilgameš’s third dream in SB IV 101: @i{ilsû šamû qaqqaru irammum}.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "IV" 101
// L I.4 SB "VII" 166

%n ana ša anzî | nāʾer || pānūšu | mašlū
#note: See @bib{RN117@305–306}: “The use of the status absolutus suggests that word @i{nāʾir} may be taken as a name of the Anzû bird (note the pairing @i{an-ze-e na-ʾ-i-ri} in the enumeration of monstrous images depicted in silver and copper by Esarhaddon at the gate of E-gašan-kalamma, the temple of Ištar-Ninlil at Arbil: @bib{RN52@33, 10}). The terrible features described in ll. 66–8 belong to the agent of Death, as is implicit in the SB version, in which the dream continues with the binding of Enkidu and his forceable abduction into the presence of Ereškigal. The fact that the figure displays the face of the Anzû bird confirms the identification, for according to a phrase quoted by a commentary on @i{Sakikku} VII, ‘Death (has) the face of Anzû’: @i{šá iqbû/qabû}@akk{{u₂}} @i{mu-ú-tu pa-ni} @akk{{d}}@i{anz}[@i{î}(anzu)@akk{{mušen}} (@i{šá-kin})] (@bib{RN2231@32 rev. 12}; cf. @bib{RN125@157}). The Vision of Kummâ attests to another tradition, in which Death has the head of a dragon: @akk{{d}}@i{mu-ú-t}[@i{u}?] @i{qaqqad mušḫušši šá-ki-in} (@bib{RN384@71, 3}); but other chthonic beings in this text have the head of Anzû (ibid., 5: Ḫumuṭ-tabal, the ferryman), or, utilizing the identical phrase as SB VII 169, his face (10: @i{ištēn eṭ-lum}, i.e., an unidentified demon, … @i{a-na ša an-zi-i pa-nu-šú ma-áš-lu}). Though there the demon Namtar and Death (@akk{{d}}@i{mūtu}) are separate, elsewhere they are equated (@i{nam-ta-ru} = @i{mu-u-tú}: @i{LTBA} II 1 v 54 // 2, 264 // 3, iv 3), and in other literature the agent of Death is often personified as Namtar, who brings death to mankind and was accordingly given a place in the pantheon as the vizier of Ereškigal (see the references collected in @i{CAD} N/1, p. 248).”
